          "heading": "Applied dossier: seepage at an excavation toe",
          "content": "## Applied dossier: seepage at an excavation toe\n\nA temporary excavation intersects a permeable sand layer above a lower-permeability silty layer. The groundwater head outside the excavation is 2.8 m above the interior control level, and the approximate vertical seepage path through a silty base is 6 m. A first screening gradient is i=2.8/6\u22480.467. This ratio is dimensionless. If a representative vertical hydraulic conductivity is 3\u00d710\u207b\u2077 m/s, the Darcy velocity magnitude is about 1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2077 m/s, equivalent to 12.1 mm/day. Darcy velocity is discharge divided by gross cross-sectional area, not the mean pore-water velocity. The latter would require division by the flow-effective porosity and a more complete transport model.\n\nSuppose mineral specific gravity is 2.66 and void ratio 0.72. The elementary saturated cohesionless critical-gradient estimate is (Gs\u22121)/(1+e)\u22480.965. A gradient of 0.467 is below this benchmark, but that fact does not certify the excavation safe from piping, heave or erosion. The gradient may be concentrated around sheet-pile tips; the actual flow path is not straight and uniform, and soil erosion can initiate below the ideal global critical gradient. Real assessments must examine filters, exit gradients, geometry, stratification and staged pumping.\n\nThe stress calculation at the base provides a second perspective. If a soil slice carries 100 kPa total vertical overburden and an unusually high upward pressure of 75 kPa at the critical point, the effective vertical stress is only 25 kPa. The skeleton therefore has much less normal confinement than a total-stress chart suggests. It is the spatial distribution and evolution of this pore pressure that matters, not a single water-table mark placed on a geological section.\n\nAn investigation proposal should include two piezometers screened at different depths and a record of pumping response. If the deeper instrument reads a higher hydraulic head, upward seepage is plausible; if equal heads are observed, the assumed pressure field must be revised. Report a range of conductivities and investigate coarse seams because an order-of-magnitude variation in a thin layer can dominate the flow. The model supports a defensible measurement plan, not a finished dewatering design."
