          "id": "sec-2-6",
          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Technical methods notebook: groundwater energy and layered media",
          "content": "## Technical methods notebook: groundwater energy and layered media\n\n**Head as energy per unit weight.** Groundwater flow is driven by spatial variation in hydraulic head. In a saturated pore-water domain the piezometric head is the sum of elevation head z and pressure head u/\u03b3w. If a piezometer screen is 3 m below a chosen datum but its water column stands 5 m above the same datum, the measured total head is 5 m; it is not \u22123 m. Explicit datums prevent false claims about upflow and downflow. Compute the difference between two screened heads and divide by path length to obtain a hydraulic gradient. Flow direction follows decreasing head, although the coordinate sign must be defined.\n\n**Darcy discharge and velocity.** The magnitude Q=k i A uses the hydraulic conductivity in m/s, dimensionless gradient and total area normal to flow in m\u00b2. The result is m\u00b3/s. If a gross cross-section is 2 m\u00b2, k=10\u207b\u2075 m/s and i=0.2, then Q=4\u00d710\u207b\u2076 m\u00b3/s. A travel-time discussion needs the effective pore velocity, which is approximately specific discharge divided by effective porosity under an appropriate flow model. The two velocities should not be labeled interchangeably. Neither relation predicts a contaminant breakthrough curve without dispersion, sorption and boundary conditions.\n\n**Layer equivalence.** Parallel layers carry flow under a common head gradient; their discharges add. The relevant equivalent horizontal conductivity is thickness-weighted arithmetic mean, kh=\u03a3kiHi/\u03a3Hi. Perpendicular layers carry the same steady discharge but divide the head loss; the effective vertical conductivity is the thickness-weighted harmonic mean, kv=\u03a3Hi/\u03a3(Hi/ki). Take a 1 m high-conductivity layer at 10\u207b\u2074 m/s and a 3 m silty layer at 10\u207b\u2076 m/s. Then kh=(0.0001\u00d71+0.000001\u00d73)/4=2.575\u00d710\u207b\u2075 m/s. The vertical equivalent is 4/(1/0.0001+3/0.000001)\u22481.329\u00d710\u207b\u2076 m/s. Treating both paths with the arithmetic mean would drastically overestimate vertical flow.\n\n**Stress consequences.** Integrate total overburden from unit-weight measurements and subtract the measured or modeled pore pressure. Under simple static groundwater, u=\u03b3w(z\u2212zw) below the water level, and zero gauge pressure is often assigned above it. Real partially saturated soils may have suction and perched water. When water heads are not hydrostatic, pressure at a given point must be obtained from its piezometric head rather than from the distance to a visual water line. That correction may alter predicted excavation stability or settlement.\n\n**Design boundary.** A critical hydraulic gradient is not a comprehensive erosion threshold. Backward erosion piping can evolve along preferential flow paths and filter gradations matter. A simplistic classroom safety ratio using an average gradient should never be used to certify an embankment, excavated base or dam exit. A site-specific seepage geometry, monitoring plan and appropriate erosion assessment are required.\n\n**Measurement task.** Sketch two piezometers at different elevations and invent physically consistent head readings for upward, downward and zero vertical flow. For each sketch, calculate the average vertical gradient, specify the sign and show what changes if the screen locations are swapped. Explain which observation\u2014the screen depth or the water level in the instrument\u2014determines hydraulic head."
