          "heading": "Applied dossier: the density result that appears to pass",
          "content": "## Applied dossier: the density result that appears to pass\n\nA roadwork crew reports a bulk field unit weight of 20.6 kN/m\u00b3 at a gravimetric water content of 11%. The laboratory maximum dry unit weight for the material is 19.4 kN/m\u00b3 under the specified reference compactive effort. Converting from bulk to dry gives \u03b3d,field=20.6/(1+0.11)\u224818.56 kN/m\u00b3. The relative compaction is 18.56/19.4\u00d7100\u224895.6%. That number can only be judged against the actual contract acceptance threshold. A universal assertion that all road subbases must reach a particular percentage is not justified.\n\nSuppose a neighboring test has almost the same bulk unit weight but water content 15%; the calculated dry unit weight is 20.6/1.15\u224817.91 kN/m\u00b3 and relative compaction is approximately 92.3%. The apparent wet density is the same but the mass of dry solids per unit volume is smaller. This comparison illustrates why reporting wet density by itself can be misleading and why moisture measurement must accompany it.\n\nThe test crew then notices that a roller has crossed the area more frequently than planned. The proper response is not to assume that additional passes automatically increase dry density. On the wet side of optimum, pore pressures and softening can limit the density gain, and a thin lift over a weak layer may deform as a system. Sample location, lift depth, particle size and gauge calibration also influence whether observed differences represent actual material changes.\n\nFor an educational sensitivity analysis, take the measured field bulk unit weight as fixed and vary assumed water content in the laboratory visualization. That is an arithmetic experiment, not a model of conditioning an embankment. A real conditioning operation changes both water content and attainable fabric and is tested empirically through trial sections. The engineering note should specify exactly what was held constant in the plotted experiment.\n\nWhere the measured percent relative compaction is close to a decision threshold, the rational next action is repeat testing under the specified sampling plan, with appropriate calibration and water-content determination. The final conclusion should report both the calculated ratio and the quality of the evidence used to obtain it."
