          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Technical methods notebook: reading and testing compaction curves",
          "content": "## Technical methods notebook: reading and testing compaction curves\n\n**How the laboratory curve is obtained.** Each point on a standard moisture-density curve reflects a prepared trial at a controlled water content and compactive effort. Moisture is measured gravimetrically, while wet mass and mold volume provide bulk density. Dry unit weight is then computed from \u03b3d=\u03b3/(1+w). The optimum water content is an empirical condition near the fitted maximum of dry unit weight for that soil and energy. It does not mean that water at that proportion gives maximum strength, minimum permeability, or minimum frost susceptibility in every field setting.\n\n**Zero-air-voids constraint.** Saturation cannot exceed 100%, so for mineral specific gravity Gs, water content w as a fraction and void ratio e, the relation Sr=wGs/e gives e\u2265wGs. At full saturation e=wGs, which implies \u03b3d,ZAV=Gs\u03b3w/(1+wGs). This curve is a theoretical upper bound for dry unit weight at each water content under the assumptions of the phase model. A test point plotting above the zero-air-voids curve signals incorrect water content, density or specific gravity data, or mismatched sample definitions. It should not be celebrated as exceptionally successful compaction.\n\n**Construct a numerical check.** Let Gs=2.70, w=0.12 and \u03b3w=9.81 kN/m\u00b3. At zero air voids the theoretical \u03b3d is 2.70\u00d79.81/(1+0.12\u00d72.70)\u224820.0 kN/m\u00b3. A laboratory maximum at 18.8 kN/m\u00b3 is physically feasible; a reported dry unit weight of 21.0 kN/m\u00b3 at the same water content would require scrutiny. Rounding Gs or w cannot typically explain such a large discrepancy. Reweigh the mold, verify the moisture sample and review whether the specimen contains a significant coarse fraction treated differently in the procedure.\n\n**From laboratory curve to field evidence.** The field technician reports \u03b3wet=19.7 kN/m\u00b3 and w=14%. Thus \u03b3d\u224817.28 kN/m\u00b3. Relative to laboratory maximum \u03b3d,max=18.8 kN/m\u00b3, relative compaction is about 91.9%. The acceptance decision depends on project specifications, the chosen reference method, the allowed moisture window and spatial sampling requirements. It is possible for a measured point to fail a specified density requirement even though it visually appears firm under equipment.\n\n**Energy is not the only control.** Lift thickness, roller contact stress, number of passes, particle degradation, moisture mixing and subgrade strength influence attained density. Different compactive effort often shifts the moisture-density curve, but the shift is material-dependent. A simple simulation parabola helps learners manipulate parameters; it is not an empirical prediction that can replace a field trial. More roller passes may be wasteful when the moisture content is the limiting factor.\n\n**Engineering exercise.** Create five fabricated laboratory specimens with increasing water content and physically feasible dry unit weights below a calculated zero-air-voids curve. Plot the values, locate an approximate maximum and mark the measurement interval. Then plan three field checks near a hypothetical marginal location and explain why sampling only the visually best section can bias the acceptance record."
