          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Applied dossier: stress beneath a footing beside a wall",
          "content": "## Applied dossier: stress beneath a footing beside a wall\n\nA 100 kN vertical point load acts at ground surface. At a depth of 3 m immediately below the load, the elastic half-space expression gives \u0394\u03c3z=3\u00d7100/(2\u03c0\u00d73\u00b2)\u22485.31 kPa. At a horizontal radius of 3 m at the same depth, multiply by [1+(3/3)\u00b2]^(\u22125/2)=2^(\u22125/2)\u22480.177, yielding roughly 0.94 kPa. This rapid decay with offset is useful for intuition; it does not claim that real foundations deliver forces through a mathematical point.\n\nA rectangular footing carrying 1,500 kN over 2.5 m\u00d73 m has average central contact pressure 200 kPa under the uniform-contact assumption. A 2:1 approximation two metres below the base would give 1,500/[(2.5+2)(3+2)]\u224866.7 kPa. Compare this heuristic with a true integration of an elastic solution before using it for detailed settlement prediction. A second footing with the same pressure but greater dimensions can influence deeper soil even when the pressure at the contact is unchanged.\n\nBeside the foundation stands a 4 m retaining wall. For dry level cohesionless backfill with \u03c6\u2032=30\u00b0, Ka\u22481/3. With \u03b3=18 kN/m\u00b3, the ideal active soil thrust is 0.5\u00d7(1/3)\u00d718\u00d74\u00b2\u224848 kN per metre of wall. A uniform 12 kPa surcharge adds (1/3)\u00d712\u00d74=16 kN/m. The soil triangular component acts H/3 above the base; the surcharge rectangular component acts H/2 above the base. They cannot be combined into one thrust magnitude and given an arbitrary lever arm.\n\nA basement wall prevented from moving may not reach active conditions, and a high water table can create an additional hydrostatic action. A credible concept sketch separates water pressure, soil effective pressure, structural surcharge and construction compaction. This prevents an overly favorable result produced by choosing a coefficient that requires wall movements the structure cannot actually undergo."
