          "secNumber": "6.6",
          "heading": "Technical methods notebook: checking pressure diagrams",
          "content": "## Technical methods notebook: checking pressure diagrams\n\n**A point load is a mathematical idealization.** For an isotropic linear elastic half-space, the Boussinesq vertical stress increment at radius r and depth z beneath a vertical surface point load Q is \u0394\u03c3z=3Q/(2\u03c0z\u00b2)[1+(r/z)\u00b2]\u207b\u2075\u141f\u00b2. Its dimensions are force divided by area, provided Q is in kN and z in metres, so the result is kPa. The r/z ratio is dimensionless. A common coding mistake is to use radial distance squared inside the power without first dividing by z\u00b2. This error may be hidden at r=0, where both formulas agree.\n\n**Check the shape.** The maximum \u0394\u03c3z at fixed z occurs at r=0; the pressure decays as lateral offset increases. At r=z, the stress equals 2\u207b\u2075\u141f\u00b2\u22480.177 times its value on the axis. At the same radial ratio, doubling the depth reduces stress under an unchanged point load by a factor of four. This symmetry provides two quick analytical tests for the plotted model. As z approaches zero, the stress approaches infinity at the point. The singularity announces the inadequacy of a point-load model close to a real footing contact patch.\n\n**Finite foundation areas.** An actual footing distributes load over area. The stress increment below the footing can be represented as an integral of point-load contributions, but the contact pressure distribution may not be uniform when loading is eccentric or the footing is flexible. A rough 2:1 distribution method is a separate engineering approximation and should not be labeled an exact Boussinesq integral. The two approaches can diverge meaningfully near footing edges and in layered ground.\n\n**Rankine pressure from stress ratio.** For cohesionless backfill, vertical smooth wall, horizontal surface and ideal active deformation, Ka=tan\u00b2(45\u00b0\u2212\u03c6\u2032/2). With uniform \u03b3, the horizontal effective stress is Ka\u03b3z at depth z, so integration from zero to wall height H produces Pa=0.5Ka\u03b3H\u00b2 per unit length. The triangular action acts H/3 above the base. A uniformly distributed surcharge q contributes Ka q at every depth, a rectangular diagram with action Ka q H at H/2. This separation is required when computing overturning moments.\n\n**Water pressure is a separate curve.** Hydrostatic water behind a wall adds a triangular distribution \u03b3w z measured from the water surface, with its own lever arm. The soil pressure used with effective strength parameters should be based on effective stress. Adding the water diagram and an earth-pressure calculation based on saturated *total* stress without proper decomposition can double-count pore effects.\n\n**Alternative states.** A basement wall held rigid may stay close to at-rest stress. Passive resistance requires more wall movement than active mobilization in many contexts. A design that silently chooses active on one side and fully mobilized passive on the other may be unconservative. The laboratory model is useful for learning thrust integration, but support movement, wall friction, groundwater and construction sequence must be judged separately."
