          "heading": "Applied dossier: sparse investigation and a misleading average",
          "content": "## Applied dossier: sparse investigation and a misleading average\n\nA proposed light industrial building sits on variable fill over natural clay. Three borings identify fill thicknesses of 1.1, 2.4 and 0.6 m. An average of 1.37 m is easy to compute, but it is not evidence of a uniform layer that is 1.37 m thick beneath every footing. The spatial transitions and origin of the fill are unknown. Before selecting one compressibility or strength parameter, the engineer should plot the borehole locations, encountered materials, sample elevations, groundwater observations and plausible alternative geological sections.\n\nFor one tube sample, advance during driving was 0.80 m and recovered length was 0.64 m. Sample recovery ratio is 0.64/0.80\u00d7100=80%. With an inside tube diameter of 75 mm and wall thickness 2 mm, the geometric area ratio is [(79\u00b2\u221275\u00b2)/75\u00b2]\u00d7100\u224810.95%. These indices are useful descriptions of the sampling operation. Neither number alone proves that undisturbed stress history or fabric has been retained in the tube.\n\nTwo oedometer specimens from the same mapped clay unit report quite different compression indexes. Rather than average immediately, compare water content, specimen condition, sampling depth, extrusion notes and tested stress range. A mixed fill, fissured crust and deeper natural clay may have been conflated as one unit. Replicate testing of a critical horizon may be more valuable than a large number of unrelated index tests.\n\nFor a sensitivity exercise, assume friction angle measurements are approximately normal with a mean of 32\u00b0 and standard deviation of 3\u00b0, solely for teaching. On a 25\u00b0 dry cohesionless slope, compute FS=tan\u03c6\u2032/tan25\u00b0 for each sampled \u03c6\u2032 using a fixed random seed. The resulting histogram or percentile curve tells you what that *assumed* distribution implies mathematically. It is not an estimate of site failure probability until the assumed statistical model and spatial representativeness have been validated.\n\nA final report should therefore distinguish observed quantities, derived parameters, correlations and judgment. It should list decisions that remain insensitive to uncertainty, decisions that may change and the least expensive additional investigation that resolves each uncertainty. The alternative is false precision: an attractive graph whose narrow confidence band has been invented by unsupported assumptions."
