          "id": "sec-8-6",
          "secNumber": "8.6",
          "heading": "Technical methods notebook: traceability and uncertainty",
          "content": "## Technical methods notebook: traceability and uncertainty\n\n**A field record is a chain of evidence.** For each borehole, record coordinates and elevation datum, start and finish date, sampling method, test depths, recovery, weather and temporary or stabilized water readings. An apparent layer boundary in an interpolated profile should be labeled an interpretation between observations, not drawn as a physically measured continuous surface. If a proposed footing crosses a suspect fill-to-clay boundary, a targeted follow-up test may have more value than many additional tests in a well-characterized corner.\n\n**Recovery and area ratio.** Recovery ratio is recovered sample length divided by advance length and is often reported as a percentage. A recovery over 100% is not impossible in a disturbed sample context, but it flags an inconsistency or special mechanism that should be explained rather than accepted without review. A thin-wall tube geometric area ratio may be defined as (Dout\u00b2\u2212Din\u00b2)/Din\u00b2\u00d7100%, provided diameters refer to the cutting geometry being evaluated. Lower ratios generally reduce displacement for an otherwise comparable geometry, but no universal inference about sample quality follows from area ratio alone.\n\n**Disturbance is physical, not merely arithmetic.** Stress relief during extraction, moisture loss, remolding, vibration, storage and extrusion alter soil structure and measured stress\u2013strain behavior. A sample that is unsuitable for oedometer interpretation may remain useful for moisture or particle-size tests. Specify the data needed before choosing sampling equipment and handling procedures; otherwise tests may be precise but answer the wrong question.\n\n**Correlations need context.** A field penetration count is affected by energy delivery, equipment, overburden and soil type. It cannot be treated as an intrinsic modulus without procedural corrections and locally relevant correlation evidence. Similar caution applies when predicting hydraulic conductivity from grain size or strength from Atterberg limits. Report estimated values and ranges, and distinguish empirical correlation error from actual subsurface variability.\n\n**Deterministic simulations of uncertainty.** A fixed-seed Monte Carlo model produces the same sampled numbers for the same input parameters. That is useful for educational reproducibility. Suppose \u03c6\u2032 is *assumed* to follow a normal distribution with mean 32\u00b0 and standard deviation 3\u00b0, and the slope has \u03b2=25\u00b0. The simulated spread in tan\u03c6\u2032/tan\u03b2 is a transformed spread from the assumed parameter distribution. It does not establish that friction angles are normal at the site, that values are spatially independent, or that the implied proportion below FS=1 estimates real-world landslide frequency.\n\n**An uncertainty register.** Separate three categories. Measurement uncertainty includes instrument calibration, recovery and test repeatability. Model uncertainty includes simplifications in loading, drainage, stress distribution and failure geometry. Spatial uncertainty includes unobserved lenses and layer changes. A practical mitigation plan identifies which decision depends on which category and chooses follow-up work accordingly. Where outcomes are sensitive to groundwater conditions, a piezometer may outperform a second index test; where outcomes depend on the thickness of weak clay, better stratigraphic coverage may be essential.\n\n**Reviewable deliverable.** Include a plan of investigation locations, logs, a longitudinal ground profile, groundwater readings by date, a table of chosen parameters with traceable sources, representative calculations, sensitivity plots and unambiguous remaining assumptions. Finish with what additional evidence would change the recommended design approach. The objective is not to remove all uncertainty, which is impossible in earth materials, but to make engineering decisions proportional to the quality and consequences of the available evidence."
